---
references:
  alpha:
    id: alpha
    type: book
    title: Alpha
  beta:
    id: beta
    type: book
    title: Beta
---

`number` and `cite` keep the **page's** scope: a renderable is not a numbering
island (D-RF-8). But a field is rendered *after* the chain has walked the
document, so its number cannot simply be the next one handed out — that would
number by **output** order, and §6.7 requires source order.

So the field's citation identities are reserved **where the field is written**.
This citation is the first one in the source, even though the field renders last:

@[Card](/docs)@@anchorA{note: ;;first in source: [^alpha];;}

Body text cites [^beta] here, so `beta` is the second identity the document
introduced — not the first:

The same identity keeps its number, whichever position cites it:

Body text cites [^alpha] again.

`%%number` belongs to the same rule: the marker is the numbering pass's, so a
head inside a payload is numbered by the pass and not by the renderer. That half
is not pinned here yet — `number` numbers elements, not identities, so the
reservation this fixture proves for `cite` does not carry over for free.
